# A spring forecast for five places in Spain, read with its chart.
lesson language-forecast v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=4 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "The weather forecast"
  goal "Read a weather forecast in [[language.name]] and answer questions from it."

  look {
    scene 32x16 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        passage forecast title="[[weather.the-weather-title]]" text="[[weather.saturday-forecast]]" width=30 numbers=true
      }
    }
    say """
      A forecast says the weather for each place. Read it one sentence at a time: the place first,
      then its weather, then the temperature in degrees, [[weather.degrees]].
      [[weather.at-night|cap]] is at night. The chart with the questions shows the same forecast as
      pictures.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.forecast-name k=4
    }
    show language.forecast-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.forecast-meaning k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show language.forecast-write k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show language.forecast-name k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=6
    show language.forecast-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.forecast-write k=5 easy-k=2 hard-k=6
    show language.forecast-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.forecast-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.forecast-write k=4
    }
  }
  language es {
    try stars=2 {
      show language.es-forecast-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    }
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.forecast-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "A forecast: the place, its weather, its degrees. [[weather.in-seville-it-is-sunny-twenty-seven-degr]]"
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is taking the weather of one place for another when the sentences
    are close together, and reading rain as cold. The forecast and the chart say the same thing, one
    in words and one in pictures, so the child can check one against the other. From this grade the
    written words are marked with their accents: a word typed without its accent is not taken. The
    dictation is read from this sheet, where it prints as Read aloud, or played by the device. If
    the child is stuck, read the forecast one sentence at a time and point at the matching column of
    the chart. Off the page, read a weather forecast online in the language, or write tomorrow's for
    your town. It meets England's key stage 2 languages programme (years 3 to 6, statutory from age
    7): "read carefully and show understanding of words, phrases and simple writing"; Japan's grades
    5 and 6 foreign languages: "Can listen to and understand concrete information regarding familiar
    and simple matters in daily life if spoken to slowly and clearly"; and CEFR A2, "Can understand
    sentences and frequently used expressions related to areas of most immediate relevance (e.g.
    very basic personal and family information, shopping, local geography, employment)". The phrases
    are in [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[weather.at-night]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[weather.it-is-raining]] and [[num.twenty-two]] and the words to write are [[weather.it-is-snowing]] and [[weather.wind]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[weather.it-is-windy]] and [[weather.no-it-is-sunny-and-warm]] and the words to write are [[weather.north]], [[weather.heat]] and [[weather.night]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
